      "created_at": "2020-05-28T18:45:33Z",
      "updated_at": "2020-05-28T18:45:33Z",
      "body": "I think this really hinges on whether we need to add random noise to report delays, since the existing API does not have any noise except to stagger queued reports at browser startup.\r\n\r\nIf we implement what is currently describe in the explainer, then this proposal doesn't seem necessary. If you get a report associated with a click you can tell which report window it came from by looking at the time delta from the click time. The only source of noise here is from browser up-time / startup staggering which hopefully should be minimal.\r\n\r\nHowever, if we feel we want to add noise to the API, we should be analyzing why we are doing it and whether reporting the `conversion-window` makes the noise less effective. For instance, in PCM noise is needed because click-time information is not known from a report and conversions are reported based on some delay relative to the conversion-time. We may consider unifying this mode of delay (see https://github.com/privacycg/private-click-measurement/issues/29 for more context) by e.g. adding noise on top of the conversion windows.\r\n\r\nIf we did something like that, the main thing we want to achieve is for PCM's privacy model to hold (i.e. your click time and your conversion time are well hidden). However, we may be able to come up with other solutions here that don't involve just adding the two delay mechanisms together.",
      "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
      "pin": null,
      "reactions": {
