      "created_at": "2020-06-05T20:14:08Z",
      "updated_at": "2020-06-05T20:14:08Z",
      "body": "Yes I think the natural way to solve this is to introduce some noise to startup that hides browser-uptime a bit more. Right now we add uniform noise to simulate a shuffle, but it might make sense to alter it to be exponential noise, with the key property that it has an unbounded tail, so a user always has _some_ plausible deniability that they got really unlucky but were actually online.\r\n\r\nRight now Chrome's uniform delay is between [0 and 5 minutes](https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/master:content/browser/conversions/conversion_policy.cc;drc=bdab6517e19d8d2e81ae45d93fd4d5109d60e195;l=89), so maybe Exponential[1/3] minutes is a reasonable (somewhat conservative) starting point so the mean delay is 3 minutes and there is only a ~3% chance a report will be delayed more than 10 minutes at startup.\r\n\r\nThe only problem with this approach is that it bunches up reports a bit more than the uniform noise we have currently (lower variance), so timing attacks may be more effective to join impressions from different publishers. We should weigh these two approaches or maybe consider summing the noise from two noise sources to get best of both worlds.",
      "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
      "pin": null,
      "reactions": {
